<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Logging</h1>
<p class="block-content">
PRADO records log messages while a request runs. A log message has a level, a category,
and text. A log router sends the collected messages to destinations such as files, a
database, the browser, or the system log. The core framework logs its own messages, and
an application adds its own.
</p>

<h2>Logging a message</h2>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>Prado::log</tt> records a message with a level and a category.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
Prado::log('Order saved', TLogger::INFO, 'app.orders');
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
Each log level also has a method that records a message at that level. When the category
is omitted, PRADO uses the name of the calling class.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
Prado::info('Order saved');       // category is the calling class
Prado::warning('Low stock');
Prado::error('Payment failed');
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The log levels are <tt>DEBUG</tt>, <tt>INFO</tt>, <tt>NOTICE</tt>, <tt>WARNING</tt>,
<tt>ERROR</tt>, <tt>ALERT</tt>, and <tt>FATAL</tt>. The methods <tt>Prado::debug</tt>,
<tt>Prado::info</tt>, <tt>Prado::notice</tt>, <tt>Prado::warning</tt>,
<tt>Prado::error</tt>, <tt>Prado::alert</tt>, <tt>Prado::fatal</tt>, and
<tt>Prado::trace</tt> each record a message at their level.
</p>

<h2>Profiling</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>Prado::profileBegin</tt> and <tt>Prado::profileEnd</tt> measure the time between two
points. Both take a token that pairs the begin with the end. <tt>profileEnd</tt> returns
the elapsed time and logs it.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
Prado::profileBegin('render');
// ... work ...
Prado::profileEnd('render');
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Routing messages</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Messages are held in memory until a route sends them to a destination. The
<tt>TLogRouter</tt> module holds the routes. Each <tt>route</tt> element sends the
messages to one destination, and the routes run in parallel.
</p>
<img src="<%~logging-routes.svg%>" class="figure" alt="Logging fan-out. Prado::log records a message into the in-memory TLogger. The TLogRouter passes the collected messages to each route. Each route applies its own Levels and Categories filter, then sends the messages that pass to its own destination, and the routes run in parallel. The routes and their destinations are TFileLogRoute to a log file in the runtime directory, TDbLogRoute to a database table, TEmailLogRoute to email, TBrowserLogRoute to the browser console, TFirebugLogRoute to the Firebug console, TFirePhpLogRoute to the FirePHP console, TSysLogRoute to the system log, and TStdOutLogRoute to standard output. TSysLogRoute and TStdOutLogRoute are since 4.3.0."/>

<h2>Log routes</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A route is a <tt>route</tt> element inside the <tt>TLogRouter</tt> module. Each route has
its own properties for its destination. PRADO provides these routes.
</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Route</th><th>Destination and properties</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TFileLogRoute</tt></td><td>A log file under the runtime directory. <tt>LogPath</tt>, <tt>LogFile</tt>, <tt>MaxFileSize</tt>, and <tt>MaxLogFiles</tt> set the file and its rotation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TBrowserLogRoute</tt></td><td>The browser's debug console. <tt>ColorizeDelta</tt> colors the time between messages.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TFirebugLogRoute</tt></td><td>The Firebug browser console. A subclass of <tt>TBrowserLogRoute</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TDbLogRoute</tt></td><td>A database table, named by <tt>ConnectionID</tt> and <tt>LogTableName</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TEmailLogRoute</tt></td><td>An email to the addresses in <tt>Emails</tt>, with <tt>Subject</tt> and <tt>SentFrom</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TFirePhpLogRoute</tt></td><td>The FirePHP browser console.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TSysLogRoute</tt> (since 4.3.0)</td><td>The operating system's log, under a <tt>Facility</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TStdOutLogRoute</tt> (since 4.3.0)</td><td>Standard output, for a command-line process.</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="block-content">
This router writes the warning, error, and fatal messages to a file, emails the fatal
messages, and stores every message in a database table.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;modules&gt;
  &lt;module id="log" class="Prado\Util\TLogRouter"&gt;
    &lt;route class="Prado\Util\TFileLogRoute" Levels="Warning, Error, Fatal"
           LogFile="app.log" MaxFileSize="1024" MaxLogFiles="10" /&gt;
    &lt;route class="Prado\Util\TEmailLogRoute" Levels="Fatal"
           Emails="ops@example.com" SentFrom="prado@example.com" Subject="Fatal error" /&gt;
    &lt;route class="Prado\Util\TDbLogRoute" ConnectionID="db" RetainPeriod="604800" /&gt;
  &lt;/module&gt;
&lt;/modules&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
An example of each route follows. Each is a <tt>route</tt> element inside the
<tt>TLogRouter</tt> module.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TFileLogRoute</tt> writes to a file under the runtime directory and rotates it by
size.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TFileLogRoute" LogFile="app.log" MaxFileSize="1024" MaxLogFiles="10" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TBrowserLogRoute</tt> writes to the browser's debug console and colors the time
between messages.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TBrowserLogRoute" ColorizeDelta="true" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TFirebugLogRoute</tt> writes to the Firebug console. It is a subclass of
<tt>TBrowserLogRoute</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TFirebugLogRoute" Levels="Warning, Error" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TDbLogRoute</tt> writes to a database table, creates the table when it is missing,
and removes rows older than the retain period in seconds.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TDbLogRoute" ConnectionID="db"
       LogTableName="prado_log" AutoCreateLogTable="true" RetainPeriod="604800" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TEmailLogRoute</tt> emails the messages to one or more addresses.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TEmailLogRoute" Levels="Fatal"
       Emails="ops@example.com" SentFrom="prado@example.com" Subject="Fatal error" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TFirePhpLogRoute</tt> writes to the FirePHP browser console under a group label.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TFirePhpLogRoute" GroupLabel="PRADO" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TSysLogRoute</tt> writes to the operating system's log under a facility, with a
prefix on each line.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TSysLogRoute" Facility="LOG_LOCAL0" SysLogPrefix="myapp" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TStdOutLogRoute</tt> writes to standard output, and limits itself to the development
web server when <tt>OnlyDevServer</tt> is set.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TStdOutLogRoute" OnlyDevServer="true" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Log levels and categories</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A route sends only the messages that pass its filters. The <tt>Levels</tt> and
<tt>Categories</tt> properties set the filters. A route with no filters sends every
message, and the two filters combine, so a message passes only when it matches both.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The log levels are <tt>Debug</tt>, <tt>Info</tt>, <tt>Notice</tt>, <tt>Warning</tt>,
<tt>Error</tt>, <tt>Alert</tt>, and <tt>Fatal</tt>. The <tt>Levels</tt> property lists the
levels the route sends.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TFileLogRoute" Levels="Warning, Error, Fatal" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Categories are hierarchical. A category is an ancestor of another when its name is a
prefix of the other. The category <tt>Prado\Web</tt> is an ancestor of
<tt>Prado\Web\UI</tt>. A route that filters on <tt>Prado\Web</tt> sends the messages of
that category and of its descendant categories. The core framework categorizes a message
by the namespace of the class that logs it, so a message logged in <tt>TPage</tt> has the
category <tt>Prado\Web\UI\TPage</tt>.
</p>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
A category in the <tt>Categories</tt> property is excluded when it begins with <tt>!</tt>
or <tt>~</tt>. The route then sends every category except that one, so the filter is set
by what to leave out instead of what to include.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;route class="Prado\Util\TBrowserLogRoute" Categories="!Prado\Web\UI\WebControls" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Flushing large logs</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
The router flushes the messages to the routes at the end of the request, and again when
the process shuts down. A long-running process fills memory with messages, so the router
flushes in batches through the <tt>onFlushLogs</tt> event. The routes send each batch and
free the memory, and the process continues.
</p>

<h2>Database logs</h2>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TDbLogRoute</tt> writes the messages to a database table, which a
<tt>ConnectionID</tt> names.
</p>

<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>RetainPeriod</tt> property removes log rows that are older than a number of
seconds, so the table does not grow without bound. The route reads and deletes the
stored logs by level, category, and time.
</p>

</com:TContent>
